Chapter 12
Working capital, and the forecast that manages it
رأس المال العامل والتنبؤ الذي يديره
The last seven chapters have been about money raised for years at a time. This one changes the horizon to weeks. Working capital is the money tied up in the ordinary running of the business — the stock on the shelves, the invoices not yet collected, the cash in the account, less what is owed to suppliers and due within the year — and it is where a profitable company most often runs into trouble.
Book 1 showed the trouble twice. Orontes, with $9,908,100 of net working capital and a cash cycle of 39.8 days, had none. Barada, growing eleven per cent a year on a sustainable 9.15 with inventory up 41 per cent, had it arrive as an overdraft. The difference between them was not profit. It was the management of the seven chapters that begin here.
Figure F612-01 is your map.
Figure F612-01. Chapter 12 roadmap: what working capital is, what it costs, and the forecast that controls it.
