WHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW   What you already know
Book 1 computed Orontes's current ratio of 3.31, its quick ratio of 2.68, its cash cycle of 39.8 days and its trade working capital of $4,544,000, and priced the funding of that trade working capital at about $272,640 a year. This chapter puts those figures into the management decision they belong to.
12.1  What working capital is
Working capital, in the broad sense, is the current assets of a business: the assets that will turn into cash within a year or within the operating cycle if that is longer. Net working capital is those assets less the current liabilities. The first measures what is tied up; the second measures how much of it the company has to fund itself after its short-term creditors have done their part.
The components are the lines of the current section of the balance sheet, and each is managed by a different chapter of this book.
Cash and equivalents, which Chapter 13 forecasts and Chapter 14 accelerates.
Marketable securities, which are cash held in a form that earns a return, and which Chapter 15 selects.
Accounts receivable, which Chapter 16 manages through credit policy.
Inventory, which Chapter 17 sizes.
Prepaid expenses, which are small and managed by nobody in particular.
Against those stand the current liabilities: accounts payable, which is supplier credit and the cheapest funding in the business; accrued liabilities; taxes payable; the current portion of long-term debt; and short-term borrowings, which Chapter 18 prices.
Two distinctions matter before any arithmetic.
Permanent against seasonal. A business that never runs out of stock always has stock, so some part of its working capital is permanently invested even though the individual items turn over constantly. That permanent core should be funded like any other long-lived asset, with long-term money. The seasonal peak above it — the extra stock before a harvest, the extra receivables after it — is temporary and should be funded temporarily. Chapter 18 makes that principle into a financing policy.
Gross against net, and trade against total. A question asking for working capital usually means current assets; one asking for net working capital means the difference. Trade working capital narrows it again to receivables plus inventory less payables, excluding cash and short-term debt, and it is the figure that measures the trading cycle rather than the funding position.
Figure F612-02 lays out the components.
