12.3  What working capital costs
Money tied up in working capital has to be financed, and the financing has a price. Book 1 computed it: $4,544,000 of trade working capital funded at the 6 per cent Orontes pays its bank costs about 4,544,000 × 0.06 = 272,640 a year.
That is the visible cost. Three more are real and less often counted.
The opportunity cost of the cash. Orontes holds $7,302,100 in a current account. At the 4 per cent a short-dated government security would pay, holding it idle forgoes 7,302,100 × 0.04 = 292,084 a year — more than the whole cost of funding the trade cycle. Chapter 15 is about not doing this.
The risk cost of the inventory. Stock can be stolen, damaged, or made obsolete by a change in demand, and insurance and storage are paid whether or not it sells. A write-down passes through cost of sales and lands on the gross margin.
The credit cost of the receivables. Some will not be paid. Orontes's allowance of $156,000 is its own estimate of that, and its credit loss expense of $63,000 is what the year actually cost.
Against those costs stand the benefits, and this is the trade-off every chapter from here to eighteen turns on. Inventory exists so that a customer who wants something can have it; cutting it too far loses sales and, at Orontes's degree of operating leverage of 4.02, a lost sale costs four times its margin in operating income. Receivables exist because customers will not buy without credit; tightening terms loses volume. Cash exists so that obligations can be met without borrowing in a hurry.
So the question is never "how little working capital can we hold" but "where is the level at which the cost of holding more exceeds the cost of holding less". Every one of the next six chapters answers that question for one component.
Figure F612-03 sets the trade-off out.
Figure F612-03. The two sides of every working-capital decision
@table
Component | Cost of holding more | Cost of holding less
Cash | Interest forgone | Borrowing in a hurry, or missing a payment
Marketable securities | A lower yield than long investments | Less liquidity when it is needed
Receivables | Funding, and credit losses | Lost sales to customers who need terms
Inventory | Funding, storage, obsolescence | Stockouts, lost sales, expedited freight
Payables | Lost settlement discounts, supplier goodwill | Funding the gap yourself
@end
