12.4  The short-term forecast
Working capital is managed by forecasting, and the instrument is the cash budget: a statement of expected receipts and payments, period by period, usually monthly for a year and weekly or daily for the nearest weeks.
What it does, and the outcome asks exactly this.
It shows when, not whether. An annual profit forecast says the year will be profitable. A monthly cash forecast says that in March the company will be $900,000 short, because the harvest is paid for in February and the customers pay in April. Only the second can be acted on.
It sizes the facilities before they are needed. A line of credit negotiated three months ahead of a seasonal peak is cheaper, larger and less conditional than one requested the week the overdraft appears. A bank asked to lend in a hurry charges for the hurry.
It prices the alternatives. Once the shortfall and its duration are known, the treasurer can compare borrowing, delaying a payment, accelerating a collection, and deferring a capital project — which is the decision Chapter 18 formalises.
It puts surplus cash to work. Knowing that $2,000,000 will not be needed for four months means it can be placed for four months rather than left overnight, which is Chapter 15's subject.
It is a control as well as a plan. Comparing the forecast with what happened reveals which assumption was wrong — collections slower than assumed, a supplier paid earlier than agreed — and improves the next forecast.
Three practices make a forecast useful rather than ornamental. A rolling horizon, re-cut every month so that twelve months are always in view rather than a budget that shortens to nothing by December. Sensitivity: a base case, and at least one case in which collections run a week slower, which is the single assumption that most often fails. And a reconciliation to the balance sheet, so that the forecast's closing receivables and inventory are the ones the sales and purchasing plans imply.
Figure F612-04 is a cash budget in outline.
Figure F612-04. The shape of a monthly cash budget
@table
Line | What it contains | Where it comes from
Opening cash | The closing balance of last month | The prior column
Receipts from customers | Sales, spread by the collection pattern | The sales forecast and the ageing
Other receipts | Interest, disposals, new borrowing | Treasury and investment plans
Payments to suppliers | Purchases, spread by the payment terms | The purchasing plan
Payroll, overheads, tax, interest, dividends | Fixed and scheduled items | The operating budget
Capital expenditure | Project payments | The capital plan
Closing cash | Opening plus receipts less payments | Computed
Surplus or shortfall against the minimum balance | The decision line | Compared with policy
@end
EXAM TRAP   A cash budget is not a forecast income statement
Sales are not receipts. A sale on 30-day terms is cash next month, and a question that gives a collection pattern is testing exactly that.
Depreciation never appears in a cash budget. Nor does any other non-cash item.
Capital expenditure and dividends do appear, although neither is in the income statement.
