Case-style practice: a forecast Barada did not make
Barada Wholesale's year ends with inventory up 41 per cent, receivables up 38 per cent, payables up 9 per cent and an overdraft of $240,000 where there was none. Sales grew 11 per cent to $21,978,000 and net income was $283,500. The owner says the overdraft "came out of nowhere in the last quarter".
Required. Explain, using working-capital arithmetic, why the overdraft was predictable from the start of the year. Say what a monthly cash forecast would have shown and when, and name the two actions the forecast would have made possible. Under 300 words.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
Answers and explanations
Section checks
SC12-1  Answer A. In the broad sense it is the current assets.
B is wrong. Current liabilities are the other side of the net figure.
C is wrong. Total assets include the plant.
D is wrong. Cash is one component.
SC12-2  Answer A. A permanent requirement is a long-term asset in substance.
B is wrong. An overdraft is repayable on demand.
C is wrong. Trade credit funds part of the cycle, not its permanent core.
D is wrong. The individual items turn over; the level does not.
SC12-3  Answer A. Trade working capital is receivables plus inventory less payables.
B is wrong. Receivables are included.
C is wrong. Inventory is included.
D is wrong. Payables are deducted, which is to say included.
SC12-4  Answer A. 14,188,100 − 4,280,000 = 9,908,100.
B is wrong. That is current assets alone.
C is wrong. That is current liabilities alone.
D is wrong. 4,544,000 is trade working capital.
SC12-5  Answer A. 4,544,000 × 0.15 = 681,600.
B is wrong. That is the whole balance, not the increment.
C is wrong. This has no basis in the figures.
D is wrong. This misplaces the decimal.
SC12-6  Answer A. The gap must be funded or the cycle shortened.
B is wrong. Stopping sales is not a working-capital policy.
C is wrong. A larger dividend makes the gap worse.
D is wrong. Growth consumes working capital rather than funding it.
SC12-7  Answer A. 4,544,000 × 0.06 = 272,640.
B is wrong. This misplaces the decimal.
C is wrong. That is the balance, not its annual cost.
D is wrong. That is 10 per cent.
SC12-8  Answer A. 7,302,100 × 0.04 = 292,084 forgone.
B is wrong. Safety has a price, and this is it.
C is wrong. This misplaces the decimal.
D is wrong. The cash is not lost, only unemployed.
SC12-9  Answer A. The level is a trade-off between two costs.
B is wrong. Minimising invites stockouts and lost sales.
C is wrong. Maximising wastes funding.
D is wrong. The average belongs to other companies' circumstances.
SC12-10  Answer A. Timing is what can be acted on.
B is wrong. An annual forecast gives the profit.
C is wrong. The tax charge is in the operating budget.
D is wrong. Depreciation is not a cash item at all.
SC12-11  Answer A. Capital expenditure is a payment.
B is wrong. Depreciation is non-cash.
C is wrong. Accrued and unpaid means no cash has moved.
D is wrong. A revaluation moves no cash.
SC12-12  Answer A. Time improves the terms.
B is wrong. Urgency raises the price.
C is wrong. The cost of borrowing reflects the circumstances of the request.
D is wrong. Facilities are routinely arranged in advance.
SC12-13  Answer A. More assets held means lower risk and a larger denominator.
B is wrong. That describes an aggressive policy.
C is wrong. The policy affects both.
D is wrong. The highest return comes from holding least, with the risk that brings.
SC12-14  Answer A. Variability, the cost of a stockout, and the cost of money.
B is wrong. The average describes other companies.
C is wrong. Auditors do not set operating policy.
D is wrong. The share price is a consequence, not an input.
Your turn
@table
Item | Answer
Net working capital | 3,900,000: 6,400,000 − 2,500,000 = 3,900,000.
Current ratio | 2.56: 6,400,000 ÷ 2,500,000 = 2.56.
Trade working capital | 2,700,000: 2,100,000 + 1,800,000 = 3,900,000, less payables of 1,200,000.
Cost of funding it at 7 per cent | 189,000: 2,700,000 × 0.07 = 189,000.
Extra funding if sales grow 20 per cent | 540,000: 2,700,000 × 0.20 = 540,000.
Retained profit available | 675,000: 900,000 × 0.75 = 675,000.
Verdict | The growth is just affordable. Retained profit of 675,000 covers the 540,000 the working capital will absorb, with 135,000 left for everything else — so the company can grow at 20 per cent for one year without external funding, and not much faster, and not at all if it also needs new plant.
@end
Multiple-choice questions
P12-01  Answer A. Current assets less current liabilities.
B is wrong. That is working capital in the broad sense.
C is wrong. That is net assets, or equity.
D is wrong. That omits most of both sides.
P12-02  Answer A. 6,400,000 − 2,500,000 = 3,900,000.
B is wrong. This adds instead of subtracting.
C is wrong. That is the current ratio times a million.
D is wrong. That is current liabilities.
P12-03  Answer A. Plant is a noncurrent asset.
B is wrong. Inventory is a current asset.
C is wrong. Receivables are a current asset.
D is wrong. Payables are a current liability and part of the net figure.
P12-04  Answer A. A permanent requirement needs permanent funding.
B is wrong. An overdraft is repayable on demand.
C is wrong. Trade credit funds part of the cycle.
D is wrong. The requirement is real and must be funded.
P12-05  Answer A. 4,544,000 × 0.15 = 681,600.
B is wrong. That is the whole balance.
C is wrong. Growth consumes working capital.
D is wrong. This misplaces the decimal.
P12-06  Answer A. Depreciation is non-cash.
B is wrong. Capital expenditure is paid in cash.
C is wrong. Dividends are paid in cash.
D is wrong. Tax is paid in cash.
P12-07  Answer A. Timing is the whole point.
B is wrong. The annual total is the same either way.
C is wrong. Depreciation belongs to neither.
D is wrong. No standard requires a cash budget.
P12-08  Answer A. Fewer assets, higher return, higher risk.
B is wrong. That describes a conservative policy.
C is wrong. The denominator of the return changes.
D is wrong. Neither extreme is always right.
P12-09  Answer A. Running out costs sales and urgent freight.
B is wrong. Storage is a cost of holding more.
C is wrong. Obsolescence is a cost of holding more.
D is wrong. Funding is a cost of holding more.
P12-10  Answer A. Both ratios are well above the usual range.
B is wrong. An aggressive policy shows low ratios.
C is wrong. A moderate policy would be nearer 2.0 and 0.5.
D is wrong. High liquidity breaches nothing.
Case-style set: a forecast Barada did not make
Why it was predictable. Barada's working capital grows with its sales, and its sales grew 11 per cent. But its receivables grew 38 per cent and its inventory 41 per cent — three to four times the rate of sales — while its payables grew only 9 per cent. On a trade cycle that Book 1 measured at 44.4 days, that combination absorbs cash every month, and it absorbs it faster than the business earns it: net income was $283,500 against a working-capital increase several times that figure. A company retaining $283,500 and consuming well over a million in the cycle has a funding gap by arithmetic, not by surprise, and the only questions were how large and when.
What a forecast would have shown, and when. A monthly cash budget built at the start of the year from the sales plan and the actual collection pattern would have shown the cash balance declining each month and crossing zero in the third quarter. By the end of the second month, the variance report would have shown collections running behind the assumption — the 38 per cent receivables growth is visible long before the year end — and the forecast would have been re-cut to show the crossing point arriving sooner.
Two actions it would have made possible. First, negotiating a facility in advance: a seasonal line arranged in month three, with the forecast as evidence, costs less and carries fewer conditions than an overdraft taken in month nine because there is no alternative. Second, acting on the cycle itself: tightening collections and slowing the stock build are both slow to take effect, which is exactly why they have to be started before the shortfall, and a forecast is the only thing that tells a company to start.
